  "body": "The spec states that it \"wilfully violates\" RFC 5322, but I can see no justifiable reason for this.  The reason given in the spec is that many people don't recognise the extended set of valid e-mail addresses, such as `some\\@thing@example.com` or `\"hello there\"@example.com`.  But some people do recognise them, and the people that don't simply won't enter them.  #1223 states that:\n\n> we should do the minimal validation that won't exclude email addresses that someone might use. You can always impose further restrictions with the pattern attribute\n\nThat is entirely true.  If a website wants to support less than the full set of valid e-mail addresses, they can do so, but the HTML spec itself should not introduce that restriction and should default to the full set of valid addresses.  The fact is that someone might use anything allowed by the spec (because it is allowed).\n\nThe only true way to validate an e-mail address is to send an e-mail to it.  Given that, the spec as-is will accept addresses that don't exist but look OK, and reject addresses that do exist but look a bit unusual.  Now there is obviously no way to stop the false positives purely in the browser, but at least the false negatives can be stopped.\n",
